## 6. Post FX: `src/components/atmosphere/AtmospherePostFX.tsx`

```tsx
<EffectComposer multisampling={0} enableNormalPass={false}>
  <Bloom mipmapBlur intensity={0.6} luminanceThreshold={0.6} radius={0.7} />
  {/* optional, low quality off: */ godRays && <RadialGlow center={glowScreenPos} strength={0.08} />}
  <ToneMapping mode={ToneMappingMode.NEUTRAL} />
  <CrimsonGrade vignetteColor={zenith} vignetteOffset={0.25} vignetteDarkness={0.75} grain={0.12} />
</EffectComposer>
```

- **Tone mapping happens once, in post.** The renderer stays on `NoToneMapping`. Inside a composer, three.js skips material-level tone mapping when rendering to render targets, so `toneMapped:false` on the sky is effectively a no-op there. I keep it for correctness anyway.
- **Why NEUTRAL** (Khronos PBR Neutral, supported by postprocessing 6.39.1): it is the identity for values below ~0.76 whose min channel is ≈0, which describes every near-pure red we use. So the sky renders **exactly as authored**. Only highlights (the hero top edge) get compressed toward white, like the white-hot edge in the reference. AgX would desaturate the crimson toward pink; ACES pushes it toward orange.
- **Bloom**: red sky luminance ≈ 0.2126·R stays under 0.6, so the sky and glow never bloom. Only the near-white top-edge rim blooms.
- **`src/effects/CrimsonGradeEffect.ts`**: one custom `Effect` (merged into a single pass) that does:
  - a **tinted vignette** with a bottom bias (mixes toward zenithColor, not black)
  - **animated film grain**, luminance-weighted so it's strongest in the midtones, blended soft-light style, with time from the composer

